 SURVEY="Reelfoot_Rift"
 YEAR=2017
 PROCESSEDBY=PAB
 PROCESSINGSOFTWARE=mtmerge/mtft/mtedit

TRANSFER FUNCTIONS HAVE BEEN ROTATED INTO GEOGRAPHIC COORDINATES.
Processing steps: Time-series: electric and
magnetic field data were collected in a geomagnetic
north and east coordinate system using wideband
magnetotelluric instrumentation (Zen receivers from
Zonge International) at each site location.
Individual magnetic and electric channel files were merged
using program Mtmerge64.exe from Zonge
International. Resulting merged binary files have been
converted to ASCII format (*.asc files).\n\n Fourier
spectra were calculated from windowed time-series
data and used to calculate cross- and auto-power
spectra via program Mtft24x64.exe from Zonge
International. Remote reference processing, in which
synchronously recording stations are processed together
to remove incoherent noise, was carried out using
all possible station combinations.\n\n Impedance
and tipper transfer functions were calculated at
each station using program MTedit64.exe from Zonge
International. System and sensor responses
(antenna.cal file) were accounted for during this process
and transfer function estimates were made for
particular frequency bandwidths for each data run
(4096 and 256 Hz sampling frequencies). Transfer
function estimates were constructed by selecting
optimal TF estimates at each period from a series of
processing runs using different reference
combinations. High (4096 Hz sampling rate) and low (256 Hz
sampling rate) frequency wideband recordings
provided TF estimates for 1000-1 Hz and 0.001-1 Hz,
respectively. So-called optimal TFs were selected based
on examination of phase slope, smooth curve
assumptions, and operator discretion. TF estimates
provided in the *.edi files and displayed in the *.png
files have been rotated to
geographic coordinates (X = North).

